1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,614
Picture a gusty afternoon.

2
00:00:02,564 --> 00:00:05,574
Trees are bending, flags are snapping.

3
00:00:05,574 --> 00:00:07,774
But up on the ridge, some of

4
00:00:07,774 --> 00:00:09,974
the wind turbines aren't turning at all.

5
00:00:10,482 --> 00:00:13,839
A still turbine on a windy day is usually the

6
00:00:13,839 --> 00:00:16,861
machine, the grid, or the engineers saying: not now.

7
00:00:16,811 --> 00:00:19,451
Let's follow the wind from blade to power line

8
00:00:19,451 --> 00:00:21,798
and find out who's saying it, and why.

9
00:00:22,304 --> 00:00:24,639
Along the way, you'll see why a little

10
00:00:24,639 --> 00:00:26,973
more wind can mean a lot more power.

11
00:00:26,923 --> 00:00:29,328
And why, on some of the windiest

12
00:00:29,328 --> 00:00:31,732
days, turbines get told to make less.

13
00:00:32,254 --> 00:00:36,795
In 2025, wind made about 10.5 percent of

14
00:00:36,795 --> 00:00:41,337
America's utility-scale electricity, the Energy Information Administration reports.

15
00:00:41,287 --> 00:00:45,115
In Iowa, it was about 58 percent of the

16
00:00:45,115 --> 00:00:48,519
state's generation, according to Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory.

17
00:00:49,020 --> 00:00:51,253
And the machines are big.

18
00:00:51,203 --> 00:00:54,964
Berkeley Lab's annual wind report for the Energy Department says

19
00:00:54,964 --> 00:00:58,725
the average new land-based turbine in 2025 was rated at

20
00:00:58,725 --> 00:01:02,486
about 3.8 megawatts, with its hub about 335 feet up

21
00:01:02,486 --> 00:01:05,119
and a rotor about 460 feet across.

22
00:01:06,092 --> 00:01:08,605
The simplest reason a turbine stands still:

23
00:01:08,605 --> 00:01:11,119
not enough wind where the blades are.

24
00:01:11,578 --> 00:01:14,049
The Energy Department notes that wind speed increases

25
00:01:14,049 --> 00:01:16,519
with height, so the breeze you feel on

26
00:01:16,519 --> 00:01:18,990
the ground isn't the wind the rotor feels.

27
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:24,364
Every turbine has a wind gauge, an anemometer, feeding a controller.

28
00:01:24,314 --> 00:01:27,536
The department says the controller typically starts the machine

29
00:01:27,536 --> 00:01:30,399
at about 7 to 11 miles an hour.

30
00:01:30,399 --> 00:01:32,631
That's the cut-in speed.

31
00:01:32,631 --> 00:01:35,246
Below it, the turbine waits.

32
00:01:35,750 --> 00:01:39,692
Low wind is also why output looks modest on paper.

33
00:01:39,642 --> 00:01:42,908
Berkeley Lab found the U.S. wind fleet had

34
00:01:42,908 --> 00:01:46,173
a capacity factor of 34 percent in 2025.

35
00:01:46,173 --> 00:01:50,344
Projects built in 2024 reached about 37.

36
00:01:50,860 --> 00:01:54,298
Capacity factor is what a plant actually produced, divided by

37
00:01:54,298 --> 00:01:57,392
the most it could have made running flat out.

38
00:01:57,342 --> 00:02:00,515
Berkeley Lab's figures already include losses from

39
00:02:00,515 --> 00:02:03,234
curtailment, downtime and ice on blades.

40
00:02:04,188 --> 00:02:06,095
So why do a few extra

41
00:02:06,095 --> 00:02:08,003
miles an hour matter so much?

42
00:02:07,953 --> 00:02:10,619
Because of one number: three.

43
00:02:11,138 --> 00:02:13,014
The power in moving air rises

44
00:02:13,014 --> 00:02:14,889
with the cube of its speed.

45
00:02:14,839 --> 00:02:18,493
As Iowa State University's wind science program puts it, if

46
00:02:18,493 --> 00:02:22,148
the wind speed doubles, the power is multiplied by eight.

47
00:02:22,648 --> 00:02:25,423
Go from 10 miles an hour to 15, and

48
00:02:25,423 --> 00:02:28,197
the wind carries more than three times the power.

49
00:02:28,147 --> 00:02:30,696
It's also why towers keep getting taller:

50
00:02:30,696 --> 00:02:32,880
higher up, the wind is faster.

51
00:02:33,390 --> 00:02:36,069
But extra power also means extra force

52
00:02:36,069 --> 00:02:38,366
on the blades, shaft and tower.

53
00:02:38,316 --> 00:02:41,530
And a generator can only use so much.

54
00:02:42,494 --> 00:02:45,831
Engineers draw all of this as a power curve: how

55
00:02:45,831 --> 00:02:48,834
much electricity a turbine makes at each wind speed.

56
00:02:48,784 --> 00:02:52,388
Above cut-in, output climbs steeply until the turbine reaches

57
00:02:52,388 --> 00:02:55,991
its rated speed, where the generator hits its maximum.

58
00:02:56,500 --> 00:03:00,417
Take a reference turbine U.S. and European researchers designed

59
00:03:00,417 --> 00:03:03,899
in 2019 to represent a modern land-based machine.

60
00:03:03,849 --> 00:03:06,579
It starts producing at about 9 miles an

61
00:03:06,579 --> 00:03:09,308
hour and hits full output at about 22.

62
00:03:09,810 --> 00:03:13,153
Above that, more wind doesn't add power.

63
00:03:13,103 --> 00:03:15,640
The pitch system turns the blades to catch

64
00:03:15,640 --> 00:03:17,861
less of it, and output stays flat.

65
00:03:18,368 --> 00:03:21,634
Keep going, and you hit the cut-out speed.

66
00:03:21,584 --> 00:03:25,985
For that reference turbine, it's about 56 miles an hour.

67
00:03:25,985 --> 00:03:29,665
The Energy Department says controllers typically shut turbines

68
00:03:29,665 --> 00:03:32,885
off once winds exceed 55 to 65.

69
00:03:33,406 --> 00:03:35,601
Then the blades feather.

70
00:03:35,551 --> 00:03:38,945
They turn so they no longer produce the force that spins

71
00:03:38,945 --> 00:03:42,030
the rotor, the rotor slows, and output drops to zero.

72
00:03:42,540 --> 00:03:44,646
Why stop so early?

73
00:03:44,596 --> 00:03:48,150
Under an international standard, the department says, most turbines

74
00:03:48,150 --> 00:03:51,704
today must be built to withstand sustained winds of

75
00:03:51,704 --> 00:03:54,863
112 miles an hour and gusts of 156.

76
00:03:54,863 --> 00:03:57,370
Cut-out sits far below that on purpose,

77
00:03:57,370 --> 00:03:59,519
to reduce the risk of damage.

78
00:04:00,026 --> 00:04:03,522
The yaw drive keeps the turbine facing the wind.

79
00:04:03,472 --> 00:04:06,253
When speeds drop, the blades unfeather, and

80
00:04:06,253 --> 00:04:09,033
power is normally flowing again within minutes.

81
00:04:09,544 --> 00:04:12,912
In March 2017, a winter storm hit Block

82
00:04:12,912 --> 00:04:16,280
Island, home of America's first offshore wind farm.

83
00:04:16,230 --> 00:04:18,852
All five turbines shut down for several hours

84
00:04:18,852 --> 00:04:21,474
while winds topped 55 miles an hour, then

85
00:04:21,474 --> 00:04:23,768
powered back up once the storm eased.

86
00:04:23,768 --> 00:04:26,163
So the windiest hour of the year can

87
00:04:26,163 --> 00:04:28,258
be a zero hour for a turbine.

88
00:04:28,258 --> 00:04:30,222
That's not a flaw.

89
00:04:30,222 --> 00:04:32,148
That's the design.

90
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:35,455
Now the surprising one.

91
00:04:35,405 --> 00:04:38,789
Sometimes the turbine is fine, the wind is perfect, and

92
00:04:38,789 --> 00:04:41,834
the grid operator still tells it to make less.

93
00:04:42,350 --> 00:04:45,788
That's curtailment, which the EIA defines as deliberately

94
00:04:45,788 --> 00:04:49,226
reducing generation below what a plant could produce.

95
00:04:49,176 --> 00:04:51,886
It happens when supply outruns demand, or when

96
00:04:51,886 --> 00:04:54,597
there aren't enough power lines to carry it.

97
00:04:55,108 --> 00:04:58,672
In the EIA's words, on very windy days in

98
00:04:58,672 --> 00:05:02,235
particular locations, transmission capacity is often insufficient to receive

99
00:05:02,235 --> 00:05:05,007
the large amount of wind power generated.

100
00:05:05,466 --> 00:05:08,234
Berkeley Lab notes that when wind is high and

101
00:05:08,234 --> 00:05:11,002
lines are full, or other plants can't ramp down

102
00:05:11,002 --> 00:05:13,770
far or fast enough, local wholesale prices can go

103
00:05:13,770 --> 00:05:16,538
negative, meaning sellers pay buyers to take their power.

104
00:05:16,488 --> 00:05:19,435
That can trigger curtailment, too.

105
00:05:19,952 --> 00:05:22,797
Why is wind often first in line?

106
00:05:22,747 --> 00:05:25,750
In the Midwest, the EIA explains, it's cheaper and

107
00:05:25,750 --> 00:05:28,754
faster to shut down and restart wind and solar

108
00:05:28,754 --> 00:05:31,089
plants than other kinds of power plants.

109
00:05:31,606 --> 00:05:33,814
How much gets curtailed?

110
00:05:33,764 --> 00:05:37,876
Across seven U.S. grid operators, Berkeley Lab counted about

111
00:05:37,876 --> 00:05:41,532
5 percent of potential wind generation in 2025.

112
00:05:41,532 --> 00:05:44,172
The Southwest Power Pool in the central

113
00:05:44,172 --> 00:05:46,812
U.S. was highest, at about 9 percent.

114
00:05:47,316 --> 00:05:51,399
Back in 2014, it was under 1 percent.

115
00:05:51,349 --> 00:05:54,556
Since then, wind's share of that region's demand has

116
00:05:54,556 --> 00:05:57,407
grown from about 12 percent to about 38.

117
00:05:57,407 --> 00:05:59,627
More wind on the same wires means

118
00:05:59,627 --> 00:06:01,846
more hours when the wires are full.

119
00:06:02,354 --> 00:06:04,230
New lines help.

120
00:06:04,180 --> 00:06:07,550
After a big transmission build-out serving West Texas wrapped

121
00:06:07,550 --> 00:06:10,919
up around 2013, Berkeley Lab notes, wind curtailment on

122
00:06:10,919 --> 00:06:14,289
the main Texas grid fell from 17 percent in

123
00:06:14,289 --> 00:06:16,910
2009 to half a percent in 2014.

124
00:06:17,866 --> 00:06:22,076
Weather can stop a turbine in a quieter way, too: ice.

125
00:06:22,536 --> 00:06:25,764
An Iowa State-led field study, published in 2021,

126
00:06:25,764 --> 00:06:28,991
looked at a wind farm in eastern China.

127
00:06:28,941 --> 00:06:32,254
Ice grew nearly a foot thick near the blade tips, and

128
00:06:32,254 --> 00:06:35,567
despite high winds, the iced turbines turned slower, often shut down,

129
00:06:35,567 --> 00:06:38,278
and lost up to 80 percent of their power.

130
00:06:38,798 --> 00:06:40,916
Then there's wildlife.

131
00:06:40,866 --> 00:06:43,507
Bats are most at risk in late summer and

132
00:06:43,507 --> 00:06:46,147
fall, and they're more active when winds are light.

133
00:06:46,147 --> 00:06:48,990
So some wind farms raise their cut-in speed on

134
00:06:48,990 --> 00:06:51,517
those nights, and the blades may be feathered.

135
00:06:52,036 --> 00:06:56,137
A 2021 review of curtailment experiments in the U.S. and Canada

136
00:06:56,137 --> 00:07:00,239
found it cut bat deaths by about 63 percent on average.

137
00:07:00,690 --> 00:07:03,893
For eagles, some sites use cameras.

138
00:07:03,843 --> 00:07:07,228
At one Wyoming wind farm, a system that slows or

139
00:07:07,228 --> 00:07:10,613
stops individual turbines when it detects an eagle at risk

140
00:07:10,613 --> 00:07:13,998
cut eagle deaths there by 63 percent, a 2021 study

141
00:07:13,998 --> 00:07:16,368
in the Journal of Applied Ecology found.

142
00:07:16,368 --> 00:07:19,085
Same number, different study.

143
00:07:20,042 --> 00:07:23,627
Sometimes a still turbine is just being serviced.

144
00:07:23,577 --> 00:07:28,335
A turbine's brake isn't like a car's, the Energy Department explains.

145
00:07:28,335 --> 00:07:30,637
It holds the rotor still after

146
00:07:30,637 --> 00:07:32,939
shutdown, which crews need for maintenance.

147
00:07:33,448 --> 00:07:36,096
So next time you pass a wind farm and

148
00:07:36,096 --> 00:07:38,449
some rotors are parked, run down the list.

149
00:07:38,399 --> 00:07:40,810
Not enough wind up high.

150
00:07:40,810 --> 00:07:43,590
Too much wind, past cut-out.

151
00:07:43,590 --> 00:07:46,907
A full grid, or prices below zero.

152
00:07:46,907 --> 00:07:48,986
Ice on the blades.

153
00:07:48,986 --> 00:07:51,371
Bats or eagles nearby.

154
00:07:51,371 --> 00:07:53,437
Or a crew at work.

155
00:07:53,958 --> 00:07:56,442
A rotor parked for an hour tells you

156
00:07:56,442 --> 00:07:58,615
about that hour, not about the machine.

157
00:07:58,565 --> 00:08:01,128
Usually, it's the machine, the grid

158
00:08:01,128 --> 00:08:03,692
or the engineers saying: not now.

159
00:08:04,196 --> 00:08:06,550
Have you ever passed a wind farm on

160
00:08:06,550 --> 00:08:08,904
a windy day with the blades standing still?

161
00:08:08,854 --> 00:08:11,264
What do you think was going on?

162
00:08:11,264 --> 00:08:13,420
Tell me in the comments.

163
00:08:13,930 --> 00:08:16,451
If you'd like to keep following the

164
00:08:16,451 --> 00:08:18,612
electron, subscribe to The Hidden Grid.

165
00:08:18,562 --> 00:08:20,673
Every source behind the numbers in

166
00:08:20,673 --> 00:08:22,784
this video is in the description.
